A||||9||theogre||Critical: Brake Upgrade? Service?||||4||
Z||000000||theogre||02-24-2001||12:44 AM||||&gt;Nomex On&lt;<P>Some of you know I've been working on issue related to Fiero heat and brake problem for a long time now...<P>I know a number of you have been following the brake upgrade math thread. Those that haven't might want to give it a read. The brake_math.xls file I posted in that thread is Excel97. The data in that file is important. If you can't read the file let me know in this thread and I'll see if I can export it to something you can read. let me know what versions of Excel or whatver you have...<P>I've gotten real friendly today with U.S. patent 4,133,178. It's the original patent, or close to it, for the master cylinder used in Fiero. (The blowoff valve in Fiero is a little diferant.) You can read this patent at the <A HREF="http://www.uspto.gov" TARGET=_blank>U.S. patent office.</A><P>With the data from this patent I now have all the info on Fiero brakes that I've been looking for. I'll be writing a new document on just how the OE brakes work, and why the OE master cylinder doesn't work right with the Grand Am brakes on the rear axle. THIS IS IMPORTANT TO ANYONE CONSIDERING PUTTING G/A CALIPERS ON THE REAR!! Even beyond the legal issues...<P>No. This isn't Hype like most of the other "brake upgrade" documents you've read.<P>Before any more of you buy into the use of the "Grand Am/Beretta" upgrade, give me some time to get this writen up and posted to the cave.<P>The new article will explain how the OE system works, including the related GM patents, and a whole bunch of other things. This article goes way beyond the general service stuff of the present cave article. It is an absolute must know for anyone planning to alter the OE system. If you have already altered the OE system this should help you understand any problems with those alterations.<P>Please, Please, Please, DO NOT alter your existing brakes until after I get the new article finished. This one will take me most of the next week to get together. I have a bunch of graphics to draw for this one. <P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||4
Z||000001||BENGAL4||02-24-2001||01:32 AM||||Ogre, me and 87ECO5, was discussing this very subject and we came to this conclusion. <BR>If you say don't do it, then we wont. Thanks for your time and effort on this subject.<P>Bill<BR>86GT <BR>3800 II S/C||||reg||1
Z||000002||danny boy||02-24-2001||04:35 AM|||| Maybe I'm jumpin the gun, but it seems to me that the logical choice would be to use the master cyl off the same car the calipers came off of, not that I really know what I'm talkin about.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||02-24-2001||02:50 PM||||Bill, Your welcome. I started this planning to do it to my own car. I may consider it again in the future but only when I think it can be done with maximum safety.<P>Danny, At first glance that would make allot of sense. But as you guessed it's allot more complicated then that.<P>If you only do the front only Grand Am upgrade the OE MC will work. The big issue with the upgrades is the rear. I'll explain all this in much more detail soon. The new article will cover the tech details of normal service as well as issues around the Grand Am calipers.<P>There's allot going on with the brakes on GM cars that isn't obvious. That step bore master cylinder is an amazing little piece of work. I didn't fully understand them until I read thru the patent on it. (If you've never read a patent... it's not allot of fun. Don't try it online. It really only come together when you can lay the thing out and see the graphics and text at the same time.) To complicate it even more there are a few versions of the MC. The diferance is in the blowoff valve and that's explained in yet other patent documents.<P>Does anyone have a spare 84-87 MC they don't want. I need one for this article. It doesn't have to be a good one. I'm going to take it apart to verify which version of valving it uses. I'd hate to spend money at the scrap yard for something I'm just going to rip apart. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-24-2001).]||||reg||1
